## Design model (SOLVED — reproduces all 16 Mtn/Speed scores exactly)

Full matrix `Q3Data.xlsx` → `Q3_Components` · model → `Q3_Design_Model` · proof → `quarters/Q3/_validate_design_model.py`

- **Speed base recipe:** aero frame · racing tires · precision brakes · basic drop-down bars · polymer gel racing seat · no carrier/suspension
- **Speed points:** 14sp (2×7) = optimal · **24sp = −3** · **7sp = −16** · **decals = +2** · reflectors = +1 · lights = +1 · **ceiling 77**
- **SWIFT BIKE FIX (2 changes → 72 to 77, ties best):** gears **24sp → 14sp (+3)** · **ADD colorful brushstroke decals (+2)**. Everything else already correct.
  - Root cause: Swift Bike inherited Hike Bike's **24-speed mountain drivetrain** — it is the ONLY Speed brand in the industry with 24sp. 14sp is also a simpler/cheaper drivetrain → likely LOWERS unit cost (verify vs component price list).
- **Mountain base recipe:** rugged frame · mountain high-grip tires · standard disc brakes · basic straight bars · 24sp · front shocks · decals · no lights
- **Mountain points:** all-purpose seat = optimal · **comfort seat = −3** · **reflectors = −1** · **ceiling 73**
- **Hike Bike = 73 of 73. VERIFIED OPTIMAL — do not touch.** (LiteTrail/Blu Tail = 70 from comfort seat; TERRAMean = 72 from adding reflectors)
- **Components are segment-dependent:** reflectors **+1 Speed / −1 Mountain** / required Rec · decals +2 Speed · lights +1 Speed, excluded Mountain. Biggest lever: **gears in Speed, seat in Mountain, brakes in Rec.**
- **Recreation ceiling 76** = MountainCruise1: comfort frame · hybrid tires · **standard disc brakes** · comfort straight bars · 7sp · comfort seat · reflectors + decals + lights + basket + front shocks. (Individual Rec penalties are underdetermined — only 4 brands.)

